  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1240 AM CDT Tue Aug 11 2026\n\nNow through This Morning: Storms and Severe Storm Potential\n\nA few showers and storms developed earlier along a nearly stationary \nwest to east boundary just south of the forecast area. As the \nboundary slowly trends northward, cannot rule out a couple attempts \nat convection over the next few hours across northeastern IA into \nsouthwestern WI. Given how the storms have largely struggled even \nwith extreme buoyancy in place, there is very low confidence in any \nsevere storm development through at least 3 AM. The lack of synoptic \nlift is likely leading to these storms struggling to become \nsustained.\n\nThe higher chance for severe storms is currently being forecast to \noccur across at least the southern half of the CWA starting near \nsunrise and lasting through at least mid morning. A complex of \nthunderstorms is anticipated to develop over eastern SD into MN/IA \nover the next couple of hours and quickly move east aided by upper \nlevel forcing. Unfortunately, timing and location of this potential \nsystem remains low confidence as CAMs have once again been very \ninconsistent with the evolution of a potential MCS. Sufficient \ninstability and shear will be in place to sustain convection and \nform severe storms with the primary hazard being damaging straight \nline wind gusts up to 70-80 mph. Heavy rainfall may also lead to a \nthreat for isolated flash flooding, especially for areas that \nreceive rainfall before the main thunderstorm line moves through. \nTiming of the severe threat unfortunately remains uncertain, but in \ngeneral storms should begin to move over the western counties by 5-8 \nAM and exit off to the east by 10 AM to 12 PM with some lingering \nshowers or general thunderstorms into the afternoon hours.\n\nThis Afternoon Through Wednesday: Periodic Storm Chances\n\nThe area should be suppressed from any storm development this \nafternoon through the evening in the wake of the morning showers and \nstorms. Cloud cover should clear out and temperatures are forecast \nto rise into the low to mid 80s this afternoon which are right at \naverage for mid August. A rinse and repeat scenario may occur for \nTuesday night into Wednesday morning as another shortwave trough \nquickly propagates east/southeast from the Dakotas towards the \nMidwest. The shortwave should trigger another round of showers or \nstorms out of the Dakotas into Nebraska and they should quickly move \neast, potentially forming another MCS. Just how far east the storms \ncan get remains uncertain at this time and may highly be dependent \non how the storms pan out later this morning. Some outlier solutions \ndo have another round of storms moving across MN/IA on Wednesday \nmorning but confidence in the storms being severe or even reaching \nthe area is extremely low at this point. Another warm day is likely \non Wednesday along with mostly dry conditions. A surface boundary \nhas the potential to set up over west central Wisconsin into central \nMinnesota which could become a focal point for afternoon into the \nevening storm development. Ingredients could be in place for severe \nstorms should any storms develop along the boundary on Wednesday.\n\nThursday into Next Week: Trending Cooler with Continued Rain Chances\n\nThe center of the H500 high pressure is forecast to shift east over \nthe Gulf coast states late this week into this weekend. The main \nridge axis appears to stay south of the forecast area during this \ntime frame which will keep the region underneath westerly flow aloft. \nThis pattern should lead to cooler conditions Friday into next week \nwith high temperatures a few degrees below average. Off and on \nchances for showers and storms is likely as shortwaves embedded in \nthe upper level flow move over the Upper Mississippi Valley during \nthis time frame. The highest chances (50-70 percent) for widespread \nrain are most notable on Saturday into Sunday as mid level moisture \ntransport peaks as a stronger wave rounds the top of the ridge.",
